  "chunks": [
    {
      "id": "chunk-quadratic-def",
      "chapterId": "chapter-quadratic-eq",
      "sourceTitle": "Mathematics Textbook - Chapter 5",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "content": "A quadratic equation is a polynomial equation of the second degree. The general form is ax² + bx + c = 0, where a, b, and c are constants and a ≠ 0. The term 'quadratic' comes from the Latin word 'quadratus' meaning square.",
      "contentType": "definition",
      "chunkOrder": 1,
      "tokenCount": 45,
      "metadata": {
        "section": "Introduction",
        "difficulty": "beginner",
        "prerequisites": ["algebra-basics"]
      },
      "formulas": [
        {
          "original": "ax² + bx + c = 0",
          "type": "equation",
          "latex": "ax^2 + bx + c = 0",
          "variables": ["a", "b", "c", "x"],
          "position": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chunk-quadratic-solution",
      "chapterId": "chapter-quadratic-eq",
      "sourceTitle": "Mathematics Textbook - Chapter 5",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "content": "The solutions to a quadratic equation ax² + bx + c = 0 can be found using the quadratic formula. Let D = b² - 4ac be the discriminant. If D > 0, there are two distinct real solutions. If D = 0, there is one repeated real solution. If D < 0, there are two complex conjugate solutions.",
      "contentType": "concept",
      "chunkOrder": 2,
      "tokenCount": 68,
      "metadata": {
        "section": "Solutions",
        "subsection": "Discriminant",
        "difficulty": "intermediate",
        "prerequisites": ["quadratic-definition"]
      },
      "formulas": [
        {
          "original": "x = (-b ± √(b² - 4ac)) / 2a",
          "type": "formula",
          "latex": "x = \\frac{-b \\pm \\sqrt{b^2 - 4ac}}{2a}",
          "variables": ["x", "a", "b", "c"],
          "position": 92
        },
        {
          "original": "D = b² - 4ac",
          "type": "expression",
          "latex": "D = b^2 - 4ac",
          "variables": ["D", "b", "a", "c"],
          "position": 200
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chunk-quadratic-example",
      "chapterId": "chapter-quadratic-eq",
      "sourceTitle": "Mathematics Textbook - Chapter 5",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "content": "Example: Solve 2x² - 7x + 3 = 0. Here a = 2, b = -7, c = 3. The discriminant D = (-7)² - 4(2)(3) = 49 - 24 = 25. Since D > 0, we have two distinct real solutions: x = (7 ± 5)/4, giving x = 3 and x = 0.5.",
      "contentType": "example",
      "chunkOrder": 3,
      "tokenCount": 55,
      "metadata": {
        "section": "Worked Examples",
        "difficulty": "intermediate"
      },
      "formulas": [
        {
          "original": "2x² - 7x + 3 = 0",
          "type": "equation",
          "latex": "2x^2 - 7x + 3 = 0",
          "variables": ["x"],
          "position": 28
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chunk-motion-def-en",
      "chapterId": "chapter-motion",
      "sourceTitle": "Physics Textbook - Chapter 2",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "content": "Motion is the change of position of an object with respect to time. In classical mechanics, we describe motion using kinematic quantities: displacement (s), velocity (v), and acceleration (a). Displacement is a vector quantity representing the change in position. Velocity is the rate of change of displacement with time. Acceleration is the rate of change of velocity with time.",
      "contentType": "definition",
      "chunkOrder": 1,
      "tokenCount": 62,
      "metadata": {
        "section": "Kinematics",
        "difficulty": "beginner"
      },
      "formulas": []
    },
    {
      "id": "chunk-motion-equations",
      "chapterId": "chapter-motion",
      "sourceTitle": "Physics Textbook - Chapter 2",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "content": "For motion with constant acceleration, we have three kinematic equations. The first relates velocity and acceleration: v = u + at, where u is initial velocity, v is final velocity, a is acceleration, and t is time. The second relates displacement and time: s = ut + (1/2)at². The third relates velocity and displacement: v² = u² + 2as. These equations are fundamental to solving motion problems.",
      "contentType": "concept",
      "chunkOrder": 2,
      "tokenCount": 76,
      "metadata": {
        "section": "Kinematic Equations",
        "subsection": "Constant Acceleration",
        "difficulty": "intermediate"
      },
      "formulas": [
        {
          "original": "v = u + at",
          "type": "equation",
          "latex": "v = u + at",
          "variables": ["v", "u", "a", "t"],
          "position": 0
        },
        {
          "original": "s = ut + (1/2)at²",
          "type": "equation",
          "latex": "s = ut + \\frac{1}{2}at^2",
          "variables": ["s", "u", "t", "a"],
          "position": 80
        },
        {
          "original": "v² = u² + 2as",
          "type": "equation",
          "latex": "v^2 = u^2 + 2as",
          "variables": ["v", "u", "a", "s"],
          "position": 160
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chunk-motion-example",
      "chapterId": "chapter-motion",
      "sourceTitle": "Physics Textbook - Chapter 2",
      "sourceLanguage": "en",
      "content": "Example: A car accelerates uniformly from rest. It reaches a velocity of 20 m/s in 5 seconds. Calculate (a) the acceleration, (b) the distance traveled. Given: u = 0 m/s, v = 20 m/s, t = 5 s. From v = u + at: 20 = 0 + a(5), so a = 4 m/s². From s = ut + (1/2)at²: s = 0 + (1/2)(4)(5²) = 50 m.",
      "contentType": "example",
      "chunkOrder": 3,
      "tokenCount": 71,
      "metadata": {
        "section": "Worked Examples",
        "difficulty": "intermediate"
      },
      "formulas": [
        {
          "original": "v = 20 m/s, t = 5 s",
          "type": "expression",
          "latex": "v = 20 \\text{ m/s}, t = 5 \\text{ s}",
          "variables": [],
          "position": 0
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "id": "chunk-quadratic-kannada",
